## Evidence 3: The `ipmitool raw ...` command on Romulus sends request to local D-Bus

I originally thought the ipmitool command was sent to `/dev/ipmi-bt-host` becuase this device exists on Romulus. To test this idea, I:
1. Stop btbridge `systemctl stop org.openbmc.HostIpmi`
2. Execute the same ipmitool command

Surprisingly, the ipmitool command still worked, which means the ipmitool on Romulus doesn't go to btbridge.

Next, I executed `busctl monitor xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Host` on Romulus and executed the same ipmitool command in another terminal connected to Romulus. One message is captured:

```
> Type=method_call  Endian=l  Flags=0  Version=1 Cookie=4  Timestamp="Thu 2026-06-25 16:10:18.320005 UTC"
  Sender=:1.79  Destination=xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Host  Path=/xyz/openbmc_project/Ipmi  Interface=xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Server  Member=execute
  UniqueName=:1.79
  MESSAGE "yyyaya{sv}" {
          BYTE 4;
          BYTE 0;
          BYTE 2;
          ARRAY "y" {
                  BYTE 33;
                  BYTE 4;
                  BYTE 1;
                  BYTE 1;
                  BYTE 1;
                  BYTE 89;
                  BYTE 1;
                  BYTE 0;
          };
          ARRAY "{sv}" {
          };
  };
```

The result showed that the `ipmitool raw ...` command seem to be sent by directly calling the following D-Bus method rather than going to the btbridge.

```
xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Host
/xyz/openbmc_project/Ipmi
xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Server.execute()
```

Then, I searchd the ipmitool source code and found out it contains code that can call the D-Bus method:

```
~/openbmc/build/romulus/workspace/sources/ipmitool/src (devtool)$ grep -nr openbmc ./*
./plugins/dbus/dbus.c:55:	static const char *destination = "xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Host";
./plugins/dbus/dbus.c:56:	static const char *object_path = "/xyz/openbmc_project/Ipmi";
./plugins/dbus/dbus.c:57:	static const char *interface = "xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Server";
./plugins/dbus/dbus.c:103:	/* The D-Bus xyz.openbmc_project.Ipmi.Server.execute interface
```

To prove my thought, I added the following debug log in `ipmitool/src/plugins/dbus/dbus.c` and executed the same ipmitool command.
The result showed that, yes, the ipmitool send the ipmi request by calling the D-Bus method.

